  :adr/body
  "## What the split showed

ADR-2608190300 gave a provider timeout its own name. Counting what stayed
behind, measured 2026-08-19:

| | |
|---|---|
| resident runs still `:internal-error` | 197 |
| of those, message `request timed out` | **192** |
| message `Connection reset` | 1 |
| message nil | 4 |
| recorded since that fix deployed | **0** |

So `:internal-error` was 97.5% one thing, and after the fix it is empty. The
five that were not timeouts are the subject here, and each turned out to be a
different way of losing the same information.

## Decision

**A transport failure that is not a timeout is the provider's, not ours.** A
`Connection reset` two tool calls into a resident tick was recorded as
`:internal-error` — the drawer a reader opens looking for OUR bugs. It is now
`:provider/network-error`, one name for the whole family (reset, broken pipe,
EOF part-way), because what a reader does about them is the same and the
message saying which it was travels alongside.

Order matters again: `HttpTimeoutException` **is** an `IOException`, so the
transport branch has to come after the timeout branches or it swallows them.
That is the third time in this series that a subclass check in the wrong order
would have merged two answers.

**An unclassified failure records what threw.** The four runs with a nil
message have nothing that identifies them — the class was never written down,
and there is no way to find out now. `:cause-class` is on `run/failed` from
here. The message is the first thing a reader wants and the first thing an
exception is allowed to omit; the class is neither.

`:provider/network-error` is not admitted to `complete-resident-no-op!`, for
the reason a timeout is not: the tick never found out.

## The test that proved nothing

The first version of the `:cause-class` test built the event map itself and
asserted on what it had just written. Reverting the production change left it
green. It is now driven through `run-goal-job!` with the provider stubbed to
throw, and reverting the change reddens it.

That is the same defect this whole series is about, committed inside a test
written to catch it, and it was found only because every change here is
reverted once to see which test notices. A test that is never broken on
purpose is a test whose colour means nothing.

## Where the timeouts actually are

With every failure named, the post-fix mix over 28 resident runs was
`:provider/timeout` 14, `:safe-no-op` 9, `failed` 4, `completed` 1. So the
classification did its job -- `:internal-error` is gone and the real problem is
in view -- and the real problem is that half the ticks time out.

Two hypotheses died against the data. It is not transcript growth late in a
run: **7 of the 14 ended with zero tool receipts and 6 never reached
`plan/recorded`**, so they timed out on the FIRST request, where the prompt is
at its smallest. And it is not the prompt, which ADR-2608190400 already
measured at ~1.5k static tokens carrying policy rather than padding.

What is left is not the queue, and the first answer written here said it was.

**Corrected the same day, by measuring instead of reasoning.** The paragraph
this replaces argued that one slot shared by 90 resident Bots and the Hermes
profiles beside them was being oversubscribed. Three measurements say no:

| measured | |
|---|---|
| slot occupancy, 60 samples over 298s | **45% busy** -- idle more than half the time |
| Hermes demand, 6h across all 23 profiles | **17 API calls**, 8 of them to murakumo (0.05/min) |
| cloud-itonami-app demand, 258 min | 62 resident runs, ~266 requests (**1.03/min**) |

Every busy sample carried `temperature 0.2`, which is this application's
signature and nobody else's. The Hermes fleet is not a competitor for this
slot; it was assumed to be one because it exists and is running, which is not
evidence.

**What the timings actually show.** The same resident first request, sent
twice minutes apart: **122.4s** and **26.6s**. On the successful one, server
side was 25.0s of the 26.6 -- prompt 2,091 tokens at 166/s for **0.9s**, output
274 tokens at 11.4/s for **24.1s**, queue 1.6s.

So output generation is 96% of the time and the prompt is 3%. That is the
third measurement to retire \"shrink the prompt\", and this one settles it.
What remains unexplained is the variance: an identical request taking 26s and
122s within minutes, against a slot that is idle 55% of the time. Intermittent
multi-minute stalls, origin not established.

⚠ The reproduction was not faithful in one respect and it is recorded rather
than buried: the request built here did not carry `disable-thinking?`, which
production sets for every resident Goal, so 643 characters of the 274 output
tokens were reasoning that production would not have generated. A faithful
first request is therefore FASTER than the 26.6s measured, which makes the
122.4s harder to explain, not easier.

**Nothing in this application is known to cause the timeouts**, and the two
changes that look obvious remain wrong: raising the limit makes a stalled
request hold the only slot longer, and the prompt is 3% of the time.

## Verification

  dropping the transport branch -> a-dropped-connection-is-not-a-fault-in-this-application
  dropping `:cause-class`       -> a-failure-records-what-threw-even-when-it-says-nothing

`the-classifier-keeps-three-failures-apart` used a broken pipe as its example
of \"anything else\" and had to change: an `IOException` stopped being anything
else when the transport branch landed. It now uses a failure that is not
transport at all."}]
